    Author            = 'Driver Automation Tool Contributors'
    Description       = '2.25.0 - Feature Branch: Introduced Cross-Model SHA-256 Content-Hashed Deduplication Engine. Deduplicates identical driver and BIOS payload files across models per manufacturer. Uses smart hardlink probing (Test-DATHardLinkSupport) on local NTFS volumes and network NAS SMB shares, creating zero-copy hardlinks when supported and falling back to standard file copy when unsupported. Keeps full Dell Command Update (DCU) DCUCatalog.xml and PnPUtil INF integrity while saving 30-50% disk storage on staging and package shares. Includes end-of-sync Deduplication & Storage Savings Summary.'tes a distinct NotApplicable status for Flash64W 3/4/5 instead of the old Installed lie. When versions can''t be compared numerically (some non-numeric vendor tags), or when the vendor utility reported NotApplicable, detection falls back to the marker - but only when the BIOSAtMarker anchor exactly equals the device''s current live BIOS, so any firmware change re-arms hardware-based detection automatically. Driver and DriverUpdates detection stay marker-based (no single hardware version exists to verify). Re-sync to restage the apply script and refresh existing apps - on the next maintenance window every device that was falsely "installed" but is actually behind on BIOS will go non-compliant and re-flash, surfacing the at-risk fleet through the deployment''s native compliance view without a separate report. 2.13.2 fixes BIOSDCU silently exiting clean when DCU''s scan returned "no applicable updates" while the device was genuinely behind on BIOS. Field firing: a Latitude/OptiPlex on BIOS 1.8.1 with the staged BIOSDCU app targeting 1.9.0 - the pre-flash check confirmed the device was behind, the DCU engine ran, and its scan returned exit-500 "everything current" anyway (DCU''s applicability rules - SystemID match, dellVersion parsing, Inventory Collector verdict, SupportedDevices PCI - disagreed with the catalog). Install-BIOSDCU treated the 0 return as success and exited without flashing, so the BIOS never moved. The DCU engine now sets $script:DCUNoApplicable when /scan reports 500, and Install-BIOSDCU explicitly falls back to Invoke-DellBIOSFlash on that signal - Flash64W''s DUP framework re-evaluates against the live device directly and is the trusted reference path the legacy BIOS deployments have always used. DriverUpdates semantics are unchanged (a 500 there genuinely is "everything current" across a 30-DUP package). Re-sync to restage the apply script. 2.13.3 extends the same fallback to DCU /applyUpdates returning 500. Field firing on a 5.6.0.17 device with BIOS 1.8.1 vs target 1.9.0: /scan succeeded with the scan gate passing one applicable BIOS update from the catalog, but /applyUpdates'' own internal re-scan declined and returned 500 ("everything current") - typical when the Dell DUP framework''s runtime conditions (AC power, battery level, pending reboot, TPM / Secure Boot state) aren''t met. The engine''s apply path now also sets $script:DCUNoApplicable on the 500 outcome, and Install-BIOSDCU falls back to Flash64W on either signal. DriverUpdates semantics are unchanged (a DUP-loop runtime check is the legacy BIOS path''s job, not the multi-driver path''s). 2.13.4 quiets two benign log noises that show up in the apply log AFTER a successful DCU run that signaled reboot-required (the normal happy-path BIOS apply). dcu-cli refuses every follow-on /configure with exit 5 ("a previous operation requires a system reboot to complete") until the device reboots, so the persistent-catalog re-point and the post-run DAT-managed re-assertion were both logging alarmingly: "Could not point resident DCU at the persistent catalog ... dell.com may remain enabled" and "DCU locked to DAT-managed mode [post-run]: applied ; not supported: scheduleManual(exit 5), scheduleAction(exit 5), ..." - which read like the run partially failed when in reality the BIOS flashed cleanly, the pre-run lockdown is still in effect, and the next engine run after reboot picks up where this one left off. Both branches now detect the "pending reboot from this run" state and log a single calm "deferred until reboot completes" line at Severity 1 instead. No behavior change to the BIOS flash itself; this is just diagnostic noise reduction. 2.13.5 fixes the Deploy Applications tab''s BIOS checkbox missing the new BIOS Updates (DCU) applications: the 2.13.0 Find-DATExistingApplications filter for Type=''BIOS'' was scoped to "BIOS Update - ..." (legacy Flash64W) and explicitly EXCLUDED "BIOS Update (DCU) - ..." so the two sync types could coexist in lookups without collision, which was the right call - but the deploy tab passes Type=''BIOS'' alone, so DCU BIOS apps never appeared in the picker. The checkbox now also requests Type=''BIOSDCU'' so both delivery flavors surface as one user-facing BIOS bucket (the engine choice was made at sync time; at deploy time the admin thinks of these as one category). 2.14.0 begins unifying driver delivery into a single apply script so one Invoke-DATApply.ps1 covers both the full-OS (Application / Intune / maintenance-window) path and OSD task sequences. The script gains an offline mode: when it runs in WinPE (auto-detected via the X: system drive or the MiniNT marker key, or forced with -Offline), Driver mode now injects the pack''s INFs into the OFFLINE image with dism.exe /Add-Driver against the target volume (-TargetPath, else the OSDTargetSystemDrive task-sequence variable, else an auto-detected fixed volume that carries \Windows) instead of pnputil into the running OS. Firmware-class INFs (ClassGuid f2e7dd72-6468-4e36-b6f1-6488f42c1b52 - Surface UEFI/SAM/ME and any OEM firmware) are skipped offline because firmware only installs in the full OS; a fast /Add-Driver /Recurse pass is used when nothing is filtered, otherwise a per-INF pass with a lenient "some-succeeded = success" policy mirroring the online pnputil path. BIOS, BIOSDCU and DriverUpdates modes are skipped with a clear note in WinPE (they require the running OS), and the detection marker / 3010 reboot signal are not used offline (the task sequence owns reboots). The full-OS behavior is unchanged and gated behind the WinPE/-Offline detection. This is the foundation for retiring the legacy Invoke-CMApplyDriverPackage.ps1; dynamic device-identity matching and package download from the ConfigMgr AdminService land in a follow-up build. 2.15.0 completes that unification and replaces the legacy Invoke-CMApplyDriverPackage.ps1 for OSD: a new -DiscoverFromAdminService switch makes the offline path self-driving. In WinPE the apply script now identifies the device (manufacturer, model, SystemSKU via root\wmi MS_SystemInformation, and the Lenovo 4-char machine type), queries the ConfigMgr AdminService (SMS_Package + SMS_DriverPackage over HTTPS, with a dedicated read-only service account or default credentials and an optional self-signed-cert bypass), selects the best driver package by matching the device SystemSKU / machine type / model against each package''s existing "(Models included:...)" description and DAT name conventions - gated by the target OS / architecture and resolved to the newest version - then downloads that package with the task sequence''s OSDDownloadContent agent and injects it via the 2.14.0 offline path. Server and credentials come from -AdminServiceServer / -AdminServiceUser / -AdminServicePassword or the TS variables DATAdminServiceServer / DATAdminServiceUser / DATAdminServicePassword, and the resolved PackageID is also written to DATDriverPackageID so a native "Download Package Content" step can be used instead. Matching reuses the metadata the sync already writes, so no package rebuild is needed. One TS step now services every model; the AdminService matching and content download require validation in a real OSD environment (they cannot be exercised in CI). 2.16.0 fixes Surface driver packages building with the wrong "(Models included:...)" SKU. Surface has no catalog SystemSKU, so Get-SurfaceDriverPack returned no SystemID and the package description fell back to the MODEL NAME (e.g. "Surface Pro 12th Edition Intel") - which never matches a device''s real SystemSKU, so neither the legacy Invoke-CMApplyDriverPackage.ps1 nor the new AdminService matcher could ever bind a Surface package to a device. OEMSources.json Surface entries gain an optional "sku" field (a string, or an array for consumer/commercial/region variants), populated from Microsoft''s Surface System SKU reference for the 2025 devices and other current models (e.g. Surface Pro 12th Edition Intel = Surface_Pro_for_Business_13in_12th_Ed_Intel_2103 + the Korea _2134 variant). Get-SurfaceDriverPack now returns those as SystemID so the description carries the real, semicolon-joined SystemSKU(s); models still missing a "sku" log a Severity-2 warning and fall back to the model name as before. Adding the SystemSKU is documented in CLAUDE.md. Models without a known SKU yet (e.g. the Surface Laptop family, older Pro/Go) still need one added to deploy via OSD matching. 2.17.0 adds an emergency stop for runaway deployments: Invoke-DATRemoveDeployments plus the standalone Scripts\Remove-DATDeployments.ps1 bulk-remove ConfigMgr deployments of DAT-managed Applications (ConfigMgr cannot pause/disable an Application deployment, so removal is the only stop). Scope by -CollectionName, explicit -Applications, and/or -Type (Drivers/BIOS/BIOSDCU/DriverUpdates/All); matching is strictly gated to the DAT naming prefixes via the new Test-DATManagedApplicationName so non-DAT applications are never touched, and an unscoped site-wide run requires an explicit -All acknowledgment. -WhatIf/-ReportOnly gives a dry-run hit list. The script also states the client-side truths on completion: policy drops at the next machine policy retrieval (up to ~60 min), a device already holding a pending restart keeps prompting until it reboots once, and devices still installing updates after removal are resident Dell Command Update acting autonomously (deploy Set-DATDcuManaged.ps1 to stop that). 2.18.0 adds a third DCU mode, ManualCloud, for tech-interactive fleets: DAT-managed lockdown points resident DCU at the persistent curated catalog with dell.com disabled, which made a technician''s GUI CHECK come back empty against the cloud. Set-DATDellCommandUpdateMode / Set-DATDcuManaged.ps1 -Mode ManualCloud restores dell.com as a valid interactive source (restoreDefaults first to clear the pinned catalog, with defaultSourceLocation=enable as the fallback on builds without it) while keeping every autonomous behavior off (scheduleManual, notify-only action, no toasts) and re-enabling autoSuspendBitLocker so tech-driven BIOS installs behave like stock DCU. The apply engine respects the ManualCloud marker across deployments: runs still clamp DCU like a managed device for their duration (engine keeps sole control; Suspend-BitLockerForFlash owns BitLocker), but the end state restores the settings backup and re-asserts the ManualCloud sequence instead of pinning the persistent catalog, and the marker is never rewritten to DATManaged. Deployment runs re-sync the apply script into package sources, so run an Invoke-DATSync after upgrading for the engine change to reach clients. 2.18.1 stops the DriverUpdates engine from trusting a DCU verdict that contradicts DCU''s own scan report. Field-confirmed on an OptiPlex 7020 Tower: dcu-cli /scan exited 500 ("No updates available") while the -report XML listed the package''s Intel PCIe Ethernet Controller DUP (20.0.3.28) - the device was genuinely behind, and a manual DCU scan installed that exact DUP the same morning; the engine had exited clean and SCCM marked the app Installed, silently holding the device on the old driver. The scan-500 path now cross-checks the report: if it lists any of THIS package''s staged DUPs, the engine distrusts the verdict and falls back to the built-in DUP engine (each DUP''s own framework re-evaluates applicability on the device; not-applicable DUPs self-skip with exit 3/4/5; per-component version markers keep later cycles cheap). Foreign-only report nodes still count as a corroborated "everything current". The applyUpdates-500-after-scan-matched path gets the same treatment for DriverUpdates (BIOSDCU already fell back to Flash64W there since 2.13.2). Run Invoke-DATSync after upgrading so package sources pick up the new apply script. 2.19.0 adds New-DATDcuModeApplication plus the standalone Scripts\New-DATDcuModeApp.ps1: packages Set-DATDcuManaged.ps1 as a ConfigMgr Application (''Dell Command Update Mode - <Mode>'') so a DCU mode is deployable and ENFORCED on a collection - detection reads the DcuManagedMode registry marker, so Required deployments re-assert the mode on devices that drift (reimage, DCU reinstall, manual flips), unlike one-shot Run Scripts pushes. Install treats the script''s exit 1 (some /configure keys unsupported on the build, marker written) as success; exit 2/3 (no DCU / too old) stay failures. DATManaged/ManualCloud apps carry an uninstall command that reverts to Dell out-of-box. Fresh deployment types get Manufacturer=''Dell Inc.'' and not-a-VM requirement rules so broad collections are safe targets. Optional -DistributionPointGroupName distributes content (refresh-only on unchanged re-runs) and -CollectionName deploys via Invoke-DATDeployApplications (Required by default). Do not deploy different modes Required to overlapping collections - each app re-asserts its own marker and the device flip-flops. 2.19.1 stops one deterministically-broken vendor installer from keeping a whole DriverUpdates application red: after 2 consecutive failures of the SAME DUP version on a device (per-component registry ledger; field case: Intel Dynamic Tuning vA07 dying in 2.6s with installer error -1 on a Dell Pro Micro), the built-in engine QUARANTINES that DUP - skipped with an advisory, not counted as failure - until a newer version ships or it succeeds once (either resets the ledger). Transient failures still get a clean retry and still report Failed until the threshold; missing-file (AV-quarantine) failures never quarantine because their fix is an admin action. Also adds a DCU health advisory: when every dcu-cli call in a run exits 2 (generic fatal error - field case: DCU 5.7.0.97 with a broken installation), the fallback to the built-in engine now names the broken DCU install / stopped Dell Client Management Service as the cause instead of leaving per-command warnings that read like tool bugs. Run Invoke-DATSync after upgrading so package sources pick up the new apply script.2.20.0 adds a Windows 10 driver-pack fallback for Windows 11 targets: Dell stopped cutting Win11 enterprise CABs for models already end-of-sale at Win11 GA (field case: XPS 13 9370 - Win11-capable, per-model DUP catalog still maintained, but DriverPackCatalog carries only its Windows10 pack), so Get-DellDriverPack now resolves the Windows 10 pack when no Windows 11 pack exists - Dell''s supported practice for Win11 imaging on those models (Win10/Win11 share the same driver model). Loudly logged, marked via the new OSFallback result property, one-directional (Win10 requests never pick a Win11 pack), and the requested OS is kept for package naming. This also unblocks the catalog-only DriverUpdates app and SystemID derivation for such models, which previously dead-ended on "cannot derive SystemID". Fixture-based Pester coverage for all four fallback cases. 2.20.1 fixes Lenovo Applications (BIOS Update, Drivers, Driver Updates) never appearing in Software Center: the hardware requirement rule matched the machine-type list (e.g. 20X3) against the ''DAT - Computer Model'' Global Condition, which reads Win32_ComputerSystemProduct.Version - on Lenovo that is the FRIENDLY model name ("ThinkPad L15 Gen 2"), never the machine type, so the rule could not be true on any real device; requirement rules AND together, so the deployment evaluated not-applicable on every client and Software Center hid the app with no error anywhere (field symptom: an L15 Gen 2 BIOS app deployed to a collection containing the device, absent from Software Center an hour later). Lenovo apps now gate on a new script-based Global Condition ''DAT - Lenovo Machine Type'' that reports the uppercased first 4 characters of Win32_ComputerSystem.Model - the same machine-type derivation the apply script''s Get-DATDeviceIdentity uses - matched OneOf against the catalog machine types. SystemSKU is deliberately not used for Lenovo gating: the device reports ''LENOVO_MT_20X3_BU_Think_FM_...'', which embeds the type but never equals it, so an exact-match rule would recreate the same never-true gate. Existing Lenovo apps SELF-HEAL on the next sync - the update path detects the unsatisfiable friendly-name rule, removes it, and attaches the machine-type rule in the same deployment-type update (one CI revision bump, idempotent; the manufacturer and VM-exclusion rules are preserved). After re-syncing, refresh Machine Policy on a client and the app appears in Software Center once requirements re-evaluate. 2.20.2 fixes the "Could not set custom return codes ... does not contain a method named ''SetPropertyValue''" warning the first 2.20.1 field sync surfaced (the Lenovo targeting repair itself succeeded; only the follow-on return-code attach failed): committing SDMPackageXML edits back to the site called SetPropertyValue on the object Get-CMApplication returns, but not every console build''s WqlResultObject exposes that method. Property writes now go through Set-DATResultObjectProperty, which tries SetPropertyValue when present, then the AdminUI SDK indexer property wrapper (StringValue), then plain property assignment - and reports every attempted mechanism''s error if all fail. Applies to both the sync''s return-code attach and Update-DATApplicationCommands. This matters for BIOS deployments: without the custom codes, a Dell Flash64W "reboot required" exit 2 or Lenovo SRSETUP 256 reads as an execution failure instead of soft-reboot success - re-run the sync after upgrading and confirm the log line "Updated return codes on <app>\Install". 2.20.3 fixes the Lenovo flash step that fired once 2.20.1 made the app actually reach devices: the apply script searched only the content root and only for the desktop-class utilities (SRSETUP64.exe / wFlashGUIX64.exe), so a ThinkPad package - whose extracted payload carries WINUPTP.EXE - always died with "No Lenovo flash utility found" (field: L15 Gen 2 at BIOS 1.66 vs target 1.74, marker written Status=Failed after BitLocker was already suspended). The utility search now recognizes WINUPTP64/WINUPTP (ThinkPad) ahead of SRSETUP64/SRSETUP* (modern ThinkCentre/ThinkStation) and wFlashGUIX64 (legacy desktop), searches recursively, and runs the utility from its own directory. WINUPTP -s exit codes 0 and 1 both count as accepted-with-reboot (the ROM flash happens across the following reboot(s)), with a 30-second settle window per Lenovo''s guidance before SCCM may reboot, and a failing exit quotes the winuptp.log tail into DATApply.log. When the content carries only the RAW vendor installer (the sync''s server-side extraction fallback ships it that way with a note that the deployment script must extract it - a promise the script never kept until now), the client extracts it with Lenovo''s documented InnoSetup switches into C:\Temp\DriverAutomationTool\LenovoBIOSExtract\<session> (self-pruning after 14 days, payload and winuptp.log left behind for diagnostics) and searches the payload. A still-empty search lists the content-root files in the error so the next field log self-documents. Re-run Invoke-DATSync so package sources pick up the new apply script; the device that logged the failure re-flashes on its next cycle because BIOS detection is hardware-aware. 2.20.4 stops a SUCCESSFUL BIOS flash from reporting Failed in Software Center. ThinkPad WINUPTP (exit 1) and Dell Flash64W (exit 2) PRESTAGE the firmware and only write the ROM during the reboot(s) that follow, and the apply script correctly exits 3010 (success, reboot required) - but ConfigMgr runs the deployment type''s detection immediately after the install returns, before that reboot, so the live Win32_BIOS.SMBIOSBIOSVersion still reads the OLD version. The 2.13.1 hardware-aware detection then reports not-installed (live < target), and ConfigMgr shows "not detected after installation" = Failed on every flash that actually worked (field: L15 Gen 2 flashed to 1.74, WINUPTP exit 1, app still red). Detection now has a reboot-pending grace: when the marker says Installed for this target and the live BIOS still exactly equals the anchor the apply script recorded at flash time (proving the firmware has not moved since staging), detection reports installed for a bounded window (72h) so the pending reboot can complete. Past the window it falls back to hardware detection, so a flash whose firmware never actually moves is still re-attempted (the install re-stages harmlessly - its first act is the same version check). NotApplicable and the non-numeric-version marker fallback are unchanged. Existing BIOS apps pick this up when the deployment type is refreshed on the next sync (the staged apply script also changed on this branch, which forces that refresh). 2.21.0 extends the catalog-only Driver Updates application type to Lenovo (previously Dell-only - selecting it for a Lenovo model just logged "Dell-only" and skipped). The sync now reads Lenovo''s per-machine-type update catalog (<MT>_Win10/11.xml, the same feed Lenovo System Update / Thin Installer consume and the same one the BIOS lookup already parses), fetches each package descriptor, and stages eligible DRIVER packages (BIOS, applications, component firmware and forced-reboot packages are excluded; firmware can be opted in) into per-package subfolders with a manifest.json carrying each package''s own silent install contract: ExtractCommand, Install command line, rc success codes, reboot type, and the positive-context _PnPID applicability rules from the descriptor''s Dependencies. Packages version as Cat.<fingerprint> with the same no-change skip as Dell. Client-side, a new Lenovo engine in Invoke-DATApply.ps1 gates each package on present PnP hardware IDs (Lenovo''s own applicability contract; fail-open if enumeration fails), extracts and runs the vendor install command from a space-free C:\Temp work dir, maps 3010/3011/1641 and reboot-type 3 to the soft-reboot signal, routes INF-type installs through pnputil, and reuses the Dell loop''s version-skip markers, two-strike quarantine, marker GC and Defender correlator. Driver exclusions apply to Lenovo package names/filenames with the same substring/wildcard semantics. 2.21.1 fixes the Lenovo engine''s %PACKAGEPATH% model after the first ThinkPad field run: Lenovo''s contract (what System Update / Thin Installer implement) is ONE writable package directory holding the downloaded payload, the extraction output, and the Install command''s working directory - many descriptors'' Install commands re-invoke the payload exe itself (e.g. "%PACKAGEPATH%\n34cm27w.exe /verysilent /DIR=%PACKAGEPATH%\TMP"). The first build kept the payload in the CM cache and pointed %PACKAGEPATH% at a separate extract dir, so every self-referencing install targeted a missing file and exited 1 in under a second while extraction-produced "setup.exe /s" commands succeeded. The engine now copies the payload into the per-package C:\Temp work dir and runs extract + install from that one directory; extraction is best-effort (a no-file extraction like Lenovo Universal Device Client''s no longer fails the package - the install command still runs; only an extraction timeout fails), and a missing install-command target is now a named error instead of an opaque cmd exit 1. Re-run Invoke-DATSync so the application refresh stages this apply script; prior failures were single strikes, below the two-strike quarantine, so all packages retry on the next deployment cycle. 2.22.0 automates vulnerable-driver exclusions so a flagged driver never needs the exclusion box edited by hand: sync-time screening now covers Lenovo packages too (extracted with the package''s own ExtractCommand under the same one-directory contract as the client engine, .sys files matched against the Microsoft blocklist, verdicts cached by payload SHA-256), and a screening hit is now AUTO-EXCLUDED by default - added to a persistent exclusion ledger (Settings\DriverExclusions.json) and skipped before it enters the manifest, on that sync and every future one. The ledger merges into the admin-typed exclusion patterns at every sync start and is managed with new cmdlets Get-DATDriverExclusion / Add-DATDriverExclusion / Remove-DATDriverExclusion - Add is also the one-command response to an apply-side Defender flag from a client log (the channel sync screening cannot see). Set -AutoExcludeVulnerableDrivers:$false (options.autoExcludeVulnerableDrivers) to restore the old advisory-only behavior. Auto-exclusions log loudly per hit and in an end-of-sync summary; affected package versions settle on the following sync. 2.23.0 closes the last unscreened path: the monolithic base Drivers packs of every make - Dell CAB, Lenovo SCCM pack, and Surface MSI (the only shape Surface ships in, and what OSD task sequences consume for all makes). After the pack tree is fully assembled (base pack plus any Dell overlay, before compression), every .sys is scanned against the same Microsoft blocklist and each match''s driver folder is pruned from the package source so neither pnputil online nor dism offline can install it (a root-level match prunes just the file). Enforcement is the per-sync scan itself - packs re-extract from vendor content and re-prune deterministically, so no ledger entry is needed and nothing is ever run by hand. AutoExcludeVulnerableDrivers off downgrades pruning to loud flagging; prune failures are flagged loudly and still counted in the vulnerable summary. Also fixes a latent matcher bug present since screening shipped: Test-DATFileAgainstBlocklist returned its match list with a no-enumerate wrapper, so callers collecting with @(...) counted an EMPTY result as one match - any payload containing any .sys screened as Vulnerable (advisory-era over-flagging; under 2.22.0 auto-exclusion it would have wrongly excluded clean drivers). Matches now emit enumerated and empty means empty. 2.24.0 adds an end-of-sync report to the GUI Progress page: a colour-coded per-model grid (errors first) showing exactly which packages were updated, skipped as already current, had nothing to package, or failed - no more combing the log after a sync to find out what happened. To feed it, Invoke-DATSync now returns a result object for EVERY selected model/type combination: failures that previously only logged (and returned nothing) come back as Status=Error rows carrying the exception message, and nothing-published outcomes (no driver pack / BIOS update for the model and OS, unresolvable Lenovo machine type, unsupported make for the selected download type) come back as Status=Warning rows - so scripted callers get the same complete accounting the GUI shows. 2.24.1 enhances WPF GUI error handling and background runspace resource management: ensures all 7 background runspaces (Refresh Models, Sync, Remove Package, Overlay Discovery, Overlay Cleanup, Deploy Applications, Intune Publish) dispose their runspaces and restore UI button states in try/finally blocks; adds Write-DATLog logging for unhandled dispatcher GUI exceptions; improves Stop Sync cancellation safety; and adds a Window Closing event handler to automatically stop and dispose active background tasks when the GUI closes.'
